1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,300
Hi!

2
00:00:01,250 --> 00:00:07,812
In this video we will walk you through our final Machine Learning project at UFABC: fifteen games are not a season.

3
00:00:07,812 --> 00:00:12,775
The idea is to predict how the NBA regular season will end when it has barely started.

4
00:00:12,775 --> 00:00:23,212
We will go through four parts: the topic, the data and its sources, the methodology and, finally, the results and the analysis, showing what we built at each step.

5
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:26,317
Part one: the topic.

6
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:29,762
It all starts with a simple question.

7
00:00:29,712 --> 00:00:32,175
Philadelphia is fourteen and three.

8
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:34,437
Is that a good team, or just luck?

9
00:00:34,437 --> 00:00:37,850
The NBA regular season has eighty-two games per team.

10
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:45,687
After about fifteen games, each team has played less than twenty percent of the season, and the November standings are still very misleading.

11
00:00:45,687 --> 00:00:59,275
For each team, the project answers how many wins it should end up with, and how likely it is to finish in its conference's top six, which means a direct playoff spot, in the play-in, seventh to tenth place, or out, eleventh to fifteenth.

12
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:03,505
In the end, the project delivers four things.

13
00:01:03,455 --> 00:01:06,667
Each team's expected wins, with an eighty percent interval.

14
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:10,930
The probabilities of top six, play-in and out, for all thirty teams.

15
00:01:10,930 --> 00:01:19,305
Four versions of the forecast, made before opening night and after five, ten and fifteen games, to show how much each block of games adds.

16
00:01:19,305 --> 00:01:28,392
And it is a live project: since the data comes straight from the NBA API, you just rerun the pipeline to update the forecast at any point in the season.

17
00:01:28,392 --> 00:01:33,555
The numbers in this video are a snapshot from November twenty-second, 2026.

18
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,742
Part two: the data and its sources.

19
00:01:39,010 --> 00:01:48,597
All the data comes from the NBA's public stats API, the stats dot nba dot com site, accessed with the nba underscore api Python library.

20
00:01:48,547 --> 00:01:50,147
We use two endpoints.

21
00:01:50,147 --> 00:02:00,860
League Game Log gives the log of every regular season game, one row per team per game, with the date, opponent, home or away, result, points and shooting stats.

22
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:05,522
We downloaded seventeen seasons, from 2009 to 2027.

23
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:12,122
And Schedule League V2 gives the current season's schedule, which is where the nine hundred fifty-two remaining games come from.

24
00:02:12,122 --> 00:02:15,285
Every file is cached in the data slash raw folder.

25
00:02:15,285 --> 00:02:21,610
The first download takes about ten minutes, at one request per second; after that, everything is local.

26
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:26,340
This is the core of the collection, in coleta dot py.

27
00:02:26,290 --> 00:02:29,965
If the season file already exists, it is read from the cache.

28
00:02:29,965 --> 00:02:36,040
If not, the function calls the NBA endpoint, filtering the regular season and team-level data.

29
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:45,077
Since the API sometimes refuses connections or times out, every call goes through a retry function that tries again with a wait that doubles after each failure.

30
00:02:45,077 --> 00:02:49,690
To update the current season, you just run the collection with the refresh current option.

31
00:02:51,010 --> 00:02:58,835
After collection, the game logs become a single table, games dot csv, with thirty-eight thousand seven hundred thirty-four rows.

32
00:02:58,785 --> 00:03:06,622
Each game appears twice, once for each team, and on the same row we join the opponent's stats, because they are needed to estimate possessions.

33
00:03:06,622 --> 00:03:12,622
The historical data has sixteen complete seasons, from 2010 to 2026.

34
00:03:12,622 --> 00:03:16,147
With thirty teams, that makes four hundred eighty team-seasons.

35
00:03:16,147 --> 00:03:18,785
Each team in each season is one observation.

36
00:03:18,785 --> 00:03:23,472
That is small for machine learning, and this limitation will shape the choice of models.

37
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:29,205
In phase two, features dot py turns the games into input variables.

38
00:03:29,155 --> 00:03:38,555
The key point is on the third line: we number each team's games and keep only the first N. N is the fewest games any team has played today, which is fifteen.

39
00:03:38,555 --> 00:03:44,955
This prevents data leakage, that is, accidentally using information that would not exist on the day of the forecast.

40
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:49,630
Even strength of schedule is computed only with games up to each team's cutoff date.

41
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:59,930
Possessions are estimated with the classic formula: field goal attempts, plus zero point four four times free throw attempts, minus offensive rebounds, plus turnovers.

42
00:04:01,250 --> 00:04:02,862
There are six features.

43
00:04:02,812 --> 00:04:05,450
Win percentage, wins divided by games.

44
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:07,087
Point differential per game.

45
00:04:07,087 --> 00:04:11,750
Net rating, the point differential per hundred possessions, which corrects for pace.

46
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:17,437
The Pythagorean expectation, which estimates the wins a team deserves from points scored and allowed.

47
00:04:17,437 --> 00:04:20,787
Strength of schedule, the opponents' average win percentage.

48
00:04:20,787 --> 00:04:23,050
And the previous season's win percentage.

49
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:31,487
Look at Philadelphia: a point eight hundred win percentage in its first fifteen games, but only point five eight six by the Pythagorean formula.

50
00:04:31,487 --> 00:04:37,925
The target is the final win percentage, not the number of wins, because three seasons had fewer than eighty-two games.

51
00:04:37,925 --> 00:04:43,100
To make it easier to read, errors are converted to wins by multiplying by eighty-two.

52
00:04:44,420 --> 00:04:48,032
Before modeling, we looked at the correlation between features.

53
00:04:47,982 --> 00:04:55,220
The three points-based ones, point differential, net rating and Pythagorean, correlate above point nine nine with each other.

54
00:04:55,220 --> 00:05:00,757
Point differential and net rating reach point nine nine nine: they are practically the same information.

55
00:05:00,757 --> 00:05:05,232
This is called multicollinearity, and it will be decisive when choosing the model.

56
00:05:06,550 --> 00:05:08,875
Part three: the methodology.

57
00:05:10,130 --> 00:05:21,280
The work is organized as a pipeline of seven Python scripts, one per phase: collection, features, models, simulation, versions, sensitivity and summary.

58
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:28,380
The projeto dot ipynb notebook calls these scripts in order and runs the whole pipeline, with explanations between steps.

59
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:35,742
The random seed is fixed at forty-two so everything is reproducible, and every figure is generated in Portuguese and in English.

60
00:05:35,742 --> 00:05:40,280
At the end, one script builds the paper's tables and another builds the slides.

61
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:43,862
This chart is the heart of the project.

62
00:05:43,812 --> 00:05:45,650
Each dot is a team-season.

63
00:05:45,650 --> 00:05:52,875
On the horizontal axis, the win percentage after fifteen games; on the vertical axis, the final win percentage.

64
00:05:52,875 --> 00:05:57,125
If the start told the whole story, the dots would sit on the dashed diagonal.

65
00:05:57,125 --> 00:05:58,000
They don't.

66
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:06,187
The fitted line has a slope of point six three: every point above five hundred at the start is worth only sixty-three percent by the end.

67
00:06:06,187 --> 00:06:11,512
This is regression to the mean: a team that starts twelve and three probably got a bit lucky.

68
00:06:11,512 --> 00:06:17,950
The standard deviation also shrinks, from point one nine after fifteen games to point one five at the end.

69
00:06:17,950 --> 00:06:21,975
Conclusion: any good forecast has to shrink the start toward the mean.

70
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:27,750
We compared two baselines, which act as a yardstick, with four models.

71
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:32,150
The first baseline assumes the team keeps its current win percentage until the end.

72
00:06:32,150 --> 00:06:36,450
The second uses the Pythagorean formula, with an exponent of fourteen.

73
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:57,512
The models are ordinary least squares linear regression; Ridge, which is linear regression with an L2 penalty on the size of the coefficients, controlled by the alpha parameter; Lasso, with an L1 penalty, which can set coefficients to zero; and a Random Forest with five hundred trees and at least ten teams per leaf, so it does not memorize the data.

74
00:06:57,512 --> 00:06:59,987
Features are standardized before training.

75
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:06,287
To avoid fooling ourselves, every metric is measured on data the model has not seen.

76
00:07:06,237 --> 00:07:14,025
We used leave-one-season-out: we remove a whole season, train on the other fifteen and predict the one left out, sixteen times.

77
00:07:14,025 --> 00:07:19,987
Splitting by season rather than by team matters, because teams in the same season share the same conditions.

78
00:07:19,987 --> 00:07:26,512
We also ran a temporal holdout: training up to 2023 and testing on the three most recent seasons.

79
00:07:26,512 --> 00:07:33,812
And Ridge's alpha is chosen in an inner validation, also split by season, so the test set never influences the choice.

80
00:07:35,130 --> 00:07:38,267
In the code, this lives in modelos dot py.

81
00:07:38,217 --> 00:07:44,905
The Tuned class runs a grid search with GroupKFold, where the groups are seasons, and measures mean absolute error.

82
00:07:44,905 --> 00:07:57,205
Instead of taking the alpha with the lowest error, we use the one-standard-error rule: among all alphas whose error is within one standard error of the best, we pick the largest, which is the simplest and most stable model.

83
00:07:57,205 --> 00:08:01,305
If two models are statistically tied, we keep the more conservative one.

84
00:08:01,305 --> 00:08:03,367
The chosen alpha was one hundred.

85
00:08:04,670 --> 00:08:10,170
The regression predicts the final win percentage, but the goal is the odds of making the top six.

86
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:14,432
For that, the Ridge forecast becomes the rating, each team's strength.

87
00:08:14,432 --> 00:08:24,970
San Antonio has a rating of point seven two seven, and Philadelphia, with the same record, only point five nine two, because its point differential is much smaller.

88
00:08:24,970 --> 00:08:32,432
A logistic regression, trained on historical games, turns the rating difference and home court into a win probability for each game.

89
00:08:32,432 --> 00:08:36,395
Between equal teams, the home team wins fifty-eight percent of the time.

90
00:08:36,395 --> 00:08:46,782
Each rating also gets random noise with a standard deviation, sigma, of point zero eight five, about seven wins, because we never know any team's true strength for sure.

91
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:52,187
This is the Monte Carlo simulation, in simulacao dot py.

92
00:08:52,137 --> 00:08:57,912
Instead of computing the probabilities with a formula, we draw the rest of the season ten thousand times.

93
00:08:57,912 --> 00:09:00,625
First, each team gets noise on its rating.

94
00:09:00,625 --> 00:09:09,225
Then, for each of the nine hundred fifty-two remaining games, the logistic model gives the home team's chance of winning, and a random number decides the game.

95
00:09:09,225 --> 00:09:14,250
Everything is vectorized in NumPy, so ten thousand seasons run in seconds.

96
00:09:14,250 --> 00:09:23,187
We add the current wins, build each conference's standings and count: a team's top six probability is the share of simulations in which it finished in the top six.

97
00:09:23,187 --> 00:09:29,437
The two NBA Cup games that do not have an opponent yet are simulated against an average team, on a neutral court.

98
00:09:30,770 --> 00:09:32,470
But can we trust these odds?

99
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:34,782
To answer that, we ran a backtest.

100
00:09:34,782 --> 00:09:43,945
For each of the sixteen past seasons, we went back to the day the last team completed fifteen games and simulated the rest, without using that season in training.

101
00:09:43,945 --> 00:09:46,332
Then we compared it with what actually happened.

102
00:09:46,332 --> 00:09:52,795
The eighty percent interval contained the actual wins in eighty-one percent of cases: the uncertainty is well measured.

103
00:09:52,795 --> 00:10:01,320
And the Brier score, which measures the error of the probabilities, was point four two, against point six six for always guessing the fixed proportions.

104
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,595
Lower is better.

105
00:10:03,910 --> 00:10:06,997
The calibration curve shows the same thing another way.

106
00:10:06,947 --> 00:10:12,272
We grouped the backtest forecasts by probability bin and compared them with the actual frequency.

107
00:10:12,272 --> 00:10:19,172
The points sit close to the diagonal: when the simulation says thirty percent, the event happens about thirty percent of the time.

108
00:10:20,490 --> 00:10:23,027
Two analyses complete the methodology.

109
00:10:22,977 --> 00:10:39,240
In the versions, the whole pipeline, with features, Ridge, game model, sigma, backtest and simulation, is redone at four dates in the season, each using only what was known that day: before opening night, and after five, ten and fifteen games.

110
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:47,027
And in the sensitivity analysis we repeat the validation with N from five to forty-one games, to see how the error drops with more information.

111
00:10:48,330 --> 00:10:51,342
Part four: the results and the analysis.

112
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:53,837
The main result.

113
00:10:53,787 --> 00:10:58,937
Keeping the current win percentage misses by seven point six wins per team, on average.

114
00:10:58,937 --> 00:11:01,437
Ridge misses by five point six.

115
00:11:01,437 --> 00:11:05,987
That is two wins fewer, and two wins often separate sixth place from seventh.

116
00:11:05,987 --> 00:11:11,187
On the holdout of recent seasons, Ridge does even better: five point three five.

117
00:11:11,187 --> 00:11:18,975
Spearman correlation, which measures whether the predicted order matches the real one, rises from point seven four to point seven eight.

118
00:11:18,975 --> 00:11:27,275
And an important detail: linear regression, Ridge, Lasso and Random Forest are tied within one standard error, a quarter of a win.

119
00:11:27,275 --> 00:11:31,987
In other words, the gain comes from shrinking the start, not from picking the algorithm.

120
00:11:33,300 --> 00:11:38,825
Here is the visual comparison between the baseline and the model, on out-of-sample predictions.

121
00:11:38,775 --> 00:11:45,925
On the left, keeping the win percentage produces predictions that are far too spread out, taking extreme starts at face value.

122
00:11:45,925 --> 00:11:51,900
On the right, Ridge compresses the predictions toward the mean, and the points sit closer to the diagonal.

123
00:11:53,230 --> 00:11:56,942
Why choose Ridge if linear regression had a slightly lower error?

124
00:11:56,892 --> 00:11:58,580
Because of the coefficients.

125
00:11:58,580 --> 00:12:07,092
With point differential and net rating almost identical, linear regression gives plus point one six to one and minus point one eight to the other.

126
00:12:07,092 --> 00:12:15,092
That would say a team with a better differential per possession is worse, which makes no sense: it is the model offsetting one coefficient with the other.

127
00:12:15,092 --> 00:12:21,017
Ridge splits the weight evenly across the three points-based features, about point zero two three each.

128
00:12:21,017 --> 00:12:23,230
Strength of schedule barely counts.

129
00:12:23,230 --> 00:12:26,942
Same error, but interpretable, stable coefficients.

130
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:31,885
The sensitivity analysis shows when the model helps most.

131
00:12:31,835 --> 00:12:39,235
With only five games, keeping the win percentage misses by thirteen point three wins, because the start is almost pure noise.

132
00:12:39,235 --> 00:12:42,210
Ridge misses by seven point two, almost half.

133
00:12:42,210 --> 00:12:47,985
As the season goes on, the two curves get closer: at forty-one games, the gap is small.

134
00:12:47,985 --> 00:12:51,660
The model is worth the most exactly when there is the least information.

135
00:12:52,990 --> 00:12:56,327
In the versions, each date's backtest shows the improvement.

136
00:12:56,277 --> 00:13:05,427
The Brier score drops from point five three in the preseason to point four nine with five games, point four four with ten and point four two with fifteen.

137
00:13:05,427 --> 00:13:09,790
The error in final wins drops from seven point eight to five point three.

138
00:13:09,790 --> 00:13:12,452
The dashed line is the reference of knowing nothing.

139
00:13:12,452 --> 00:13:21,952
Coverage of the eighty percent interval stays near eighty from the five-game version on; in the preseason it is eighty-seven, because the model knows little and the intervals are wide.

140
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:26,310
And you can see teams changing tiers across the versions.

141
00:13:26,260 --> 00:13:38,285
Memphis, coming off twenty-five wins, started eleven and four with a strong point differential: it went from thirty-three to forty-eight expected wins, and its top six chance went from fourteen to forty-eight percent.

142
00:13:38,285 --> 00:13:47,222
Toronto, on the other hand, rose to forty-nine in the five-game version and fell to thirty-eight in the fifteen-game one: a good start that did not last.

143
00:13:47,222 --> 00:13:50,072
That is why you cannot trust a single snapshot.

144
00:13:51,390 --> 00:13:53,265
This is the current projection.

145
00:13:53,215 --> 00:13:58,515
In the heatmap, each row is a team and each column a final position, from one to fifteen.

146
00:13:58,515 --> 00:14:02,490
The number is the share of simulations in which the team finished in that position.

147
00:14:02,490 --> 00:14:07,190
The solid line marks the top six cutoff, and the dashed line the play-in cutoff.

148
00:14:07,190 --> 00:14:15,415
At the top, the distributions are concentrated; in the middle of the table they spread over several positions, which is where the real uncertainty is.

149
00:14:16,730 --> 00:14:17,992
Some highlights.

150
00:14:17,942 --> 00:14:25,505
Boston has a ninety-seven percent chance of a top six finish, and San Antonio ninety-five, with fifty-nine expected wins.

151
00:14:25,505 --> 00:14:28,380
And Philadelphia, at fourteen and three?

152
00:14:28,380 --> 00:14:31,130
At that pace it would win about sixty-seven games.

153
00:14:31,130 --> 00:14:41,355
The model projects fifty-two point four, with an interval from forty-four to sixty, because a plus two point eight per game differential belongs to a good team, not a title contender.

154
00:14:41,355 --> 00:14:45,580
The wins already banked count; what the model shrinks is the pace from here on.

155
00:14:45,580 --> 00:14:52,955
Boston, with a worse record, has a better chance: a plus ten point five per game differential and a strong previous season.

156
00:14:52,955 --> 00:14:57,967
Memphis is on the edge, with forty-eight percent top six and forty-six percent play-in.

157
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:00,437
What we learned.

158
00:15:00,387 --> 00:15:05,000
Validating by season, without leakage, is what makes the numbers trustworthy.

159
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:14,162
A simple linear model tied with the random forest: with four hundred eighty observations and an almost linear relationship, a complex model has nothing more to learn.

160
00:15:14,162 --> 00:15:18,637
And testing the simulation on the past is what shows the probabilities make sense.

161
00:15:18,637 --> 00:15:29,887
The limitations: injuries, trades and roster changes are not modeled, the rating uses only each team's first fifteen games, and ties are broken at random rather than by the official rules.

162
00:15:29,887 --> 00:15:35,125
These are the next steps, along with new versions at twenty, twenty-five and thirty games.

163
00:15:36,430 --> 00:15:38,667
To wrap up, here is what we built.

164
00:15:38,617 --> 00:15:43,080
The code, with the notebook that runs the whole pipeline and the seven scripts.

165
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:51,042
An IEEE-format paper, in Portuguese and English, written in LaTeX, with tables and figures generated straight from the results.

166
00:15:51,042 --> 00:15:57,505
The fourteen-slide presentation, in both languages, with the talk script and a guide that explains every term.

167
00:15:57,505 --> 00:16:01,605
And the results folder, with the tables, the figures and a summary.

168
00:16:01,605 --> 00:16:11,455
The code and texts were written with help from Claude, by Anthropic; the authors defined the question, the data, the models and the validation, and checked the results.

169
00:16:12,770 --> 00:16:16,782
The takeaway is this: trust the record, but shrink it first.

170
00:16:16,732 --> 00:16:24,257
The start of the season carries information, but it has to be pulled toward the mean and combined with point differential and the previous season.

171
00:16:24,257 --> 00:16:27,820
And since the project is live, you just rerun it to update.

172
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:29,045
Thanks for watching!
